      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on this picture: the natural log curve, and log base two of x. Logs like these are how we hear. Double the frequency of a guitar note, and it climbs one octave. So the octaves you've climbed are log base two of the frequency ratio. Make a sound ten times as powerful, and its sound level rises by one bel, which is ten decibels. So the rise in bels is log base ten of the power ratio. Let's add log base ten of x as well. All three pass through the same point, one, zero, because any base to the power zero is one. But look at how they leave that point. Log base two climbs the steepest, and log base ten is the flattest. So how does the base set the slope, and by exactly how much?"
    },
