      "pronunciationText": "Let's use it. Differentiate y equals log base two of x, then find the gradient at x equals four. The base is two, so d y by d x is one over, x times the natural log of two. At x equals four, that's one over, four times the natural log of two. Keep it exact like that, or round it to about point three six one. That's the slope we measured at the start. On the guitar, x equals four is two octaves above the open string, at concert A. Up there, each extra hertz raises the pitch only ay quarter as much as it does at the open string."
